WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.299 --> 00:00:01.839
This is very tragic.

00:00:01.840 --> 00:00:07.320
It's not only tragic to the refugees but rather
tragic to humanity,

00:00:07.320 --> 00:00:10.820
to our understanding about who we are.

00:00:14.960 --> 00:00:19.660
Ai Weiwei is arguably the best known contemporary
artist alive today.

00:00:19.660 --> 00:00:23.940
Years of beatings, detention, and house arrest
by the Chinese government

00:00:23.949 --> 00:00:26.390
only fueled his fame in the West.

00:00:26.390 --> 00:00:28.429
Now living in exile in Germany,

00:00:28.429 --> 00:00:31.899
the artist has shifted focus from the repressions
of his homeland

00:00:31.899 --> 00:00:34.210
to the global refugee crisis.

00:00:34.210 --> 00:00:38.480
My experience shared a lot of similarities.

00:00:40.460 --> 00:00:43.960
In the year I was born my father was exiled.

00:00:43.960 --> 00:00:48.940
As a poet he was forbidden to write for 20
years.

00:00:48.940 --> 00:01:00.740
I grew up in a condition where I was being
completely discriminated and being mistreated.

00:01:03.040 --> 00:01:06.620
And yeah, it is very similar to a refugee’s condition.

00:01:06.620 --> 00:01:11.970
The artist’s debut feature film, Human Flow,
which will be released in the U.S. this week,

00:01:11.970 --> 00:01:15.620
was shot in dozens of refugee camps in over
20 countries.

00:01:15.620 --> 00:01:21.390
It chronicles the physical and emotional journeys
of some of the world’s 65 million refugees

00:01:21.390 --> 00:01:23.200
as they flee their homelands.

00:01:24.240 --> 00:01:29.780
It's going to be a big challenge to recognize
that the world is shrinking

00:01:29.780 --> 00:01:33.840
and people from different religions, different
cultures,

00:01:33.840 --> 00:01:39.340
are going to have to learn to live with each
other.

00:01:47.140 --> 00:01:49.110
Coinciding with the release of the film

00:01:49.110 --> 00:01:53.040
Weiwei is unveiling a major public art project
in New York.

00:01:53.040 --> 00:01:56.270
Erecting hundreds of symbolic barriers around
the city.

00:01:56.270 --> 00:01:57.920
It’s about territory.

00:01:57.920 --> 00:02:01.820
It’s about borders.

00:02:01.820 --> 00:02:04.100
It’s about immigration.

00:02:04.100 --> 00:02:12.740
And of course it has something to do with
the current condition to limit people,

00:02:12.740 --> 00:02:19.900
a travelling ban, or build the new border
to limit immigration

00:02:19.900 --> 00:02:23.220
or people even to travel.

00:02:23.220 --> 00:02:26.560
Government restrictions on the rights of individuals
to travel

00:02:26.560 --> 00:02:29.820
is a major theme in Weiwei’s work and life.

00:02:29.820 --> 00:02:37.000
My passport has been in the possession of
Chinese government for years.

00:02:37.000 --> 00:02:43.020
By refuse to me to have my passport limit
my freedom to travel.

00:02:43.020 --> 00:02:50.700
As artist I would have shows in world wide
institutions I cannot really attend.

00:02:50.700 --> 00:02:58.700
So trying to reduce my voice or my possibility
for creativity.

00:02:58.700 --> 00:03:04.040
In 2015, the Chinese government returned Weiwei’s
passport, freeing him to leave China.

00:03:04.040 --> 00:03:06.560
Recently he’s been traveling to promote
his film

00:03:06.560 --> 00:03:09.960
and oversee the installation of his work in
New York City—

00:03:09.960 --> 00:03:14.060
a freedom of movement the artist wants none
of us to take for granted.

